Sorry for the delay.

I'm not 100% sure I understand exactly what you're saying, but from the sounds of it you need:

1. Move away from reactiveValues and observeEvent where possible. Watch/do the "Effective Reactive Programming" tutorial vids here <https://www.rstudio.com/resources/webinars/shiny-developer-conference/> (sorry, it's a couple of hours--but I think you will save a lot of time in the long run).
2. Use Shiny Modules <https://shiny.rstudio.com/articles/modules.html>, they're exactly intended for this purpose.
3. Figure out how to wire up the Shiny modules to each other. I haven't (yet) done a great job of documenting this kind of thing but you'll essentially want:

# in the server function
filtered <- callModule(selectorModule, "selector", reactive(predat))
callModule(preModule, "pre", filtered)
callModule(diffModule, "diff", filtered)
callModule(crossCorrModule, "crossCor", filtered)

where the selectorModule server function is something like:

selectorModule <- function(input, output, session, fullData) {
  filteredData <- reactive({
    df <- fullData()
    # I'm imagining a checkbox for filtering instead
    if (input$filter) {
      df <- df[1:40,]
    }
    df
  })

  output$table <- renderTable(filteredData())

  return(filteredData)
}

The fundamental idea is that you pass reactive expressions back and forth between modules. By passing and returning them by name (i.e. "filteredData" instead of "filteredData()") it's like passing a reference to the reactive expression itself, rather than just passing the current value of a reactive expression.

