You can see that this error is due to a missing class and .jfindclass is failing to find it. This is probably due to a misconfiguration of Java or the Java class path. The variable driverClass[1] that's being searched for is passed to RJDBC::JDBC() by you, so you need to make sure that the class you're specifying exists in the JVM's classpath. When I call JDBC for Teradata with the driver .jar files stored in a package mypackage, I do:

JDBC(driverClass = "com.teradata.jdbc.TeraDriver", classPath = c(system.file("jdbc", "terajdbc4.jar", package = "mypackage"), system.file("jdbc", "tdgssconfig.jar", package = "mypackage")), identifier.quote = "\"")

So you need to make sure a) that the driver .jar files are accessible to the JVM, b) that the .jar files are appearing in the classPath properly, and c) that the driverClass is being specified correctly.

